Let me analyze:
- A "good" array of length r has adjacent differences all equal to 1 (including |ar - a1| = 1)
- A "great" array is non-decreasing, has values in [1,m], length ≤ n, and can form between 1 and k distinct good arrays when rearranged

The key challenge is that checking |ar - a1| = 1 means elements can't all be consecutive values. A cycle with adjacent difference 1 is very restrictive - it essentially needs to alternate between two consecutive values.

